docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:14:The ORCHESTRA-OS paper reports a controlled, pre-kernel simulation. The maintained `orchestra_paper_cpu_demo/orchestra_paper_cpu.c` is a real-process userspace approximation: it observes a real Linux host and asks processes to sleep, yield, change affinity, throttle themselves, or consume CPU. Linux CFS/EEVDF still performs final dispatch. Neither this ADR nor a passing userspace test establishes a Linux scheduling-class implementation.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:22:- temporal stability requires an explicit `S4` term;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:24:- a controller must use an actuator with a causal path to the deficient submetric;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:25:- the outer controller must update more slowly than the inner learner;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:27:- difference rewards must provide population credit without displacing the local directive signal.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:33:### 2.1 Scope and version identifiers
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:35:The C signal carries numeric state schema version `2`. The machine-readable CSV contract is maintained separately as `orchestra.paper_cpu.metrics/v2`. The program prints signal- and state-schema numbers, mode, seed, and run configuration to diagnostics; the CSV rows do not carry a state-schema or metric-schema identifier themselves.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:37:The canonical action set remains exactly:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:43:Signal payload validation requires finite normalized state and confidence values and bounded controller values before a worker accepts the frame. This ADR does not redefine the signal-frame byte contract.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:45:### 2.2 Directive, prediction fallback, and state schema v2
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:57:In ORCHESTRA mode, `decision_cpu` is predicted CPU only when confidence is finite and at least `0.50`; otherwise it is observed CPU. Baseline mode always uses observed CPU. The chosen value is clamped to `[0, 1]`.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:86:q_index = state_id * 5 + action_id
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:91:Workers start with zeroed Q-tables in fresh anonymous shared memory. No policy persistence, in-place v1-to-v2 conversion, or resume path exists. A frame with a state-schema version other than `2` is rejected before use. Because every worker is compiled into the same executable, the current program does not negotiate heterogeneous per-worker schemas.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:97:Population utility is the exact, zero-preserving geometric mean of action-dependent components:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:100:G(a_vector) = (S2 * S3 * S4)^(1/3)
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:103:For worker `i`, the counterfactual replaces that worker's action with fixed baseline action `YIELD` while retaining the population size:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:113:L_i = +0.6, if selected_action == directive
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:115:L_i -= 0.4, if thermal > 0.90 and selected_action == RUN
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:116:L_i -= switch_penalty, if selected_action != previous_action
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:133:The final reward is not globally clamped: thermal and switching penalties can make an incorrect action's reward lower than `-0.60`. The C stores the final reward together with an atomic frame-sequence stamp. A worker consumes it only when the stamp matches the action transition being updated, then clears the stamp; an absent, stale, or already consumed reward does not update the Q value. The program does not emit local, raw difference, clamped difference, final reward, or reward-sequence telemetry.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:139:Each worker exposes action, previous action, alive/exempt/fallback flags, and accepted sequence through a per-worker decision seqlock. `compute_metrics` omits a worker if that coherent snapshot cannot be obtained, if it is not alive, or if it is real-time exempt. It does not create one globally simultaneous snapshot across all workers.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:143:CSV column. Metrics remain observable when acceptance is incomplete so S1 can
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:144:show lost reach, but reward assignment and outer-controller action are gated on
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:147:Let `N` be the resulting eligible count. Fallback workers are included in the action metrics and are separately counted.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:149:The implemented signal score is:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:155:signal_reach = mean(exp(-0.7 * (frame.sequence - worker.accepted_sequence)))
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:156:S1 = clamp(freshness * accuracy * frame.confidence * signal_reach, 0, 1)
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:159:The sequence gap is floored at zero. When `N == 0`, `signal_reach` is `1`.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:164:S2 = matching_selected_actions / N
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:167:S3 = 1 - H / ln(5)
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:169:S4 = 1 - changed_actions / N
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:172:For entropy, `p_a` is the fraction selecting canonical action `a`. Normalized entropy is defined as zero when `N <= 1`, making `S3 = 1`. When `N == 0`, the C reports `S2 = S3 = S4 = 1`; it emits `eligible_workers = 0` but no separate sample-validity flag. `S4` uses every eligible worker in the denominator, including a worker's first observed action transition; there is no separate `N_temporal` cohort.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:174:S3 is whole-population action entropy, not a state-conditioned comparison among similarly situated workers. Unanimous incorrect behavior can therefore have `S3 = 1`; exact `S2 = 0` makes aggregate Q zero.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:180:Q = exp((ln(S1) + ln(S2) + ln(S3) + ln(S4)) / 4), otherwise
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:183:The helper clamps every factor to `[0, 1]`; its clamp maps non-finite values to zero. It does not insert an epsilon floor. Stable unanimous wrong action and a unanimous mass switch therefore both produce exact `Q = 0` through `S2 = 0` and `S4 = 0`, respectively.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:187:The controller runs only in ORCHESTRA mode, only when `tick % 20 == 0`, and only for a complete current-frame acceptance sample. A scheduled update is skipped rather than allowing a rejected or incomplete frame to change actuators or Q-table consensus. It uses the instantaneous `S3` and `S4` values computed at that tick, not a rolling 20-tick mean.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:189:The controller step is one-based in the maintained C. `controller_step` starts at zero, is incremented before the first update at tick 20, and is then passed to:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:192:beta_step = 0.02 / sqrt(1 + controller_step)
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:201:| `S4 < 0.82` | jitter `+= 0.20 * beta`; otherwise `-= 0.05 * beta` | `[jitter_min, 0.20]` |
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:202:| `S3 < 0.82` or `S4 < 0.82` | switching penalty `+= 0.15 * beta`; otherwise `-= 0.04 * beta` | `[0.00, 0.30]` |
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:203:| `S3 < 0.82` | consensus blend `+= 0.10 * beta`; otherwise `-= 0.03 * beta` | `[0.00, 0.15]` |
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:205:Calibration computes `1.5 * sigma_observation` and caps it at `0.20`. `controller_update` defensively clamps the supplied floor into `[0, 0.20]`; a floor greater than `0.20` therefore yields `jitter_min = 0.20` rather than rejecting the configuration. Jitter is always clamped between that effective floor and `0.20`.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:207:For each actuator, the controller first computes a raw requested value. Its saturation flag is true only if that raw value lies below the lower bound or above the upper bound; an exactly-on-bound request is not flagged. It then clamps the applied next value.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:209:The metrics and rewards for tick `t` use the actuator values published at tick `t`. On a controller tick, the new jitter and switching values are retained for publication at tick `t+1`. Consensus is different: the program immediately attempts consensus at tick `t` using the newly computed blend. CSV output distinguishes applied actuator values from `next_*` values.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:211:The controller does not act on `S1`, `S2`, or aggregate Q and does not retune predictor gain, noise covariance, prediction horizon, or sampling interval. Predictor gain is selected once during the pre-run calibration trace. Confidence below `0.50`, rather than an `S1` controller branch, switches the ORCHESTRA directive input to observed CPU.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:215:The operational controller path passes a blend in `[0, 0.15]`. The consensus helper itself does not clamp an arbitrary direct caller's blend.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:227:and Q-table reads used for action selection. A worker publishes that flag and
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:233:The convex runtime blend preserves the population mean of each Q cell. Consensus can execute on a controller tick even when instantaneous `S3` is healthy, because an existing positive blend decays gradually rather than becoming zero immediately. `consensus_applied` reports only whether the blend completed; it does not distinguish zero blend, no eligible participants, or timeout.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:237:### 2.7 Emitted telemetry
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:239:The version-2 CSV currently emits:
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:242:- observed, predicted, and decision CPU;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:243:- whether prediction was used, confidence, forecast error, and frame age;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:245:- selected-action counts, eligible-worker count, and fallback-worker count;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:246:- `S1`, `S2`, `S3`, `S4`, and Q;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:249:- controller-updated flag, one-based step, reason (`NONE`, `S3`, `S4`, or `S3+S4`), and beta;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:255:The current CSV does not emit per-worker state or accepted sequence, the internal current-acceptance count or an explicit controller-skip reason, raw/requested actuator values, bound values, saturation streaks, consensus participant count or timeout reason, reward components, Q-table values, fallback entry/exit reasons, action syscall outcomes, or final Linux dispatch outcomes. The startup diagnostic prints seed and signal/state schema numbers; those identifiers are not repeated in each CSV row.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:261:- a rolling-window controller input matching the paper's rolling-average description;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:262:- consecutive-saturation counters, sustained-saturation alerts, rollback, and controller disable/re-entry state;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:263:- an explicit non-finite controller-input rejection path with a last-known-good controller vector;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:264:- per-worker state/action schema advertisement and selective consensus disablement for heterogeneous schemas;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:266:- state-conditioned S3 for similarly situated processes;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:267:- one globally coherent population snapshot or explicit incomplete-snapshot telemetry;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:269:- a conventional-scheduler fallback after signal expiry - the current worker records fallback and selects userspace `THROTTLE`;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:270:- verified action outcomes, migration failures, affinity results, and Linux dispatch outcomes;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:271:- independently held-out predictor calibration, parameter artifact versioning, and recalibration triggers;
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:273:- kernel, NUMA, node, or cluster controller semantics; and
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:276:These limitations must not be described as completed functionality merely because the state, metric, reward, and controller unit tests pass.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:280:The maintained C implements the userspace mechanics described above and exercises real processes. The supported claim is limited to a userspace prototype whose selected paths are covered by unit and short integration tests. It does not establish kernel correctness, hard-real-time guarantees, experimental performance superiority, distributed coordination, production security, or deployment readiness.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:286:Rejected because memory pressure changes the directive but was absent from the table state, and strict boundary values could share a bucket with observations requiring another action.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:298:Rejected. A raw product changes scale as dimensions are added, and an epsilon floor reports nonzero coordination when a required component is exactly zero.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:300:### Drive the controller from aggregate Q or retune predictor gain online
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:304:### Use rolling controller inputs
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:306:Deferred rather than implemented. A rolling input would more closely match the paper's narrative and could reduce single-snapshot sensitivity, but its window, latency, and stability effects require a versioned experiment.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:312:### Update the outer controller every tick
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:314:Rejected because it would collapse the intended separation between the inner learning process and outer controller.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:318:The paper reports that directive-matched reward improved compliance, directive-aligned buckets removed a structural state ceiling, `S4` exposed synchronized mass switching, geometric-mean aggregation restored cross-version scale, and the three actuator classes target `S3` and `S4` deficits more directly than predictor retuning.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:320:The maintained source implements state schema version 2, zero-preserving geometric means, a `[-0.60, +0.60]` difference clamp, one-based decaying controller steps, actuator bounds and saturation flags, coherent per-worker decision snapshots, and bounded consensus timeout behavior.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:322:The unit suite characterizes strict directive boundaries, all 30 state buckets over boundary-adjacent samples, epsilon decay, zero-preserving metric cases, three-worker difference-reward cases, controller mapping/rates/bounds, predictor helpers, and consensus blending/timeout. The integration suite performs short baseline, adaptive, tamper, cadence, CSV-contract, and teardown checks. These tests support implementation correctness for exercised cases; they are not repeated benchmark evidence or statistical experimental validation.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:328:- Accepted frames carry state schema version 2 and bounded normalized inputs before workers index their tables.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:329:- The difference clamp and switching bound preserve the directive action's reward ordering under the implemented reward terms.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:332:- Invalid or expired signal service eventually records fallback and selects userspace `THROTTLE`; this is not the full conventional-scheduler fallback required by the research program.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:334:- Selected action and userspace approximation are not proof of final kernel dispatch or successful migration.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:341:Difference-reward computation is approximately O(N^2) in eligible workers. Metric collection performs bounded per-worker snapshot retries. Consensus is O(N * 150) per completed blend and may wait up to approximately 200 ms for active Q-table accesses, which can exceed short signal intervals; the program records aggregate missed publication deadlines but not consensus wait duration.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:343:CSV telemetry adds formatted output each tick. The present unit and integration tests do not quantify disabled/enabled instrumentation overhead, tail latency, fairness, migration cost, cache effects, or energy. No performance conclusion follows from this ADR.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:349:All workers in one run must use the same executable and table shape. Mixed v1/v2 consensus is unsupported rather than dynamically negotiated. The CSV schema is version 2 in the external schema and experiment manifest; comparisons with older sample CSV files require explicit schema qualification.
docs/adr/0001-userspace-state-metric-controller-contract.md:357:No ADR is superseded. A future ADR must supersede this one if it changes directive thresholds, state dimensions, reward weights or bounds, S1-S4 definitions, aggregation, controller inputs or mapping, cadence, step indexing, actuator bounds, consensus behavior, telemetry semantics, or policy migration.
