docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:7:Stage 6 proved the sched_ext BPF scheduler could load, enable, and schedule opted-in tasks with RUN and YIELD. Stage 7 extends this to a full two-way bridge: the userspace controller publishes validated directives through versioned BPF maps, and the BPF scheduler reads, validates, and executes all five ORCHESTRA actions with bounded behavior.
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:11:The userspace controller resides outside the kernel trust boundary. The BPF scheduler validates every directive read from userspace-populated maps:
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:12:- Bridge magic and schema version must match
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:16:- Controller state must permit the requested action
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:18:Invalid, stale, or unverifiable directives produce safe RUN fallback and telemetry only. No invalid directive may influence scheduler behavior beyond the fallback path.
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:20:## Bridge versioning
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:25:| Format version | 1 |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:26:| Schema version | 1 |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:37:| `bridge_telemetr` | ARRAY | uint32=0 | `bridge_telemetry` | 1 | 40-counter telemetry v2 |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:44:4. Control map generation is incremented (checked arithmetic)
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:45:5. Active slot is updated, generation published atomically
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:46:6. BPF reads control first, validates magic/schema/generation
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:47:7. BPF reads the active slot only when generation matches control
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:49:A partial or failed inactive-slot write never becomes active because the control generation is not advanced.
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:53:Three-component identity: TGID, PID, task start-time cookie (`/proc/[pid]/stat` field 22, `p->start_boottime` in BPF). Cookie zero disables the check for legacy compatibility. PID reuse is mitigated by the cookie: a new task with the same PID will have a different start time.
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:57:Reuses Stage 5 policy validator. Invalid policy preserves previous active generation. BPF never parses policy files.
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:63:| RUN | `scx_bpf_dispatch(p, SCX_DSQ_LOCAL, slice, flags)` |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:65:| MIGRATE | CPU validation, SCX_DSQ_LOCAL dispatch with target recording |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:66:| THROTTLE | Reduced slice dispatch with per-task throttle-until timestamp |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:76:| ROLLBACK | RUN fallback | reject | reject | reject | reject |
docs/adr/0010-userspace-bpf-signal-bridge.md:82:- SLEEP capability requires kernel support for deferred BPF task dispatch
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:7:The previous ORCHESTRA-OS userspace prototype implemented a bounded multi-actuator feedback controller but operated as a memoryless reactive function. While it successfully adjusted jitter, switching penalties, and consensus blends based on recent metrics, it could not persistently track degradation, handle long-term saturation, or safely roll back from severe parameter drift. Furthermore, invalid frames (due to tampering, missing telemetry, or timeouts) risked corrupting the control loop history.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:9:This document defines an explicitly stateful, observable, fail-safe control subsystem for the ORCHESTRA-OS signal architecture.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:13:The controller introduces six explicit, mutually exclusive states. All transitions and time spent in each state are measurable.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:15:1. **NORMAL**: The controller receives valid accepted frames and applies adaptive updates according to the scheduled cadence and mathematical bounds.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:16:2. **DEGRADED**: Coordination metrics (e.g., S3_global, S4) have persistently remained below the acceptable threshold (0.82) despite control efforts. Control updates continue, but the degradation is tracked for severity.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:17:3. **SATURATED**: At least one actuator has hit its mathematical bound, and the targeted metric deficit persists beyond the specified saturation persistence limit. The controller warns that its authority is exhausted.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:18:4. **DISABLED**: The controller has encountered repeated critical faults (e.g., invalid frames exceeding a threshold) or unrecoverable oscillation/saturation. No adaptive changes are applied.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:19:5. **ROLLBACK**: Following a critical saturation or oscillation event, the controller atomically restores the last-known-good actuator vector to stabilize the population.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:20:6. **RECOVERY**: A temporary quarantine state following a rollback, wherein the controller observes coordination quality without applying new adaptive changes. If metrics stabilize, it transitions back to `NORMAL`. Otherwise, it disables itself or rolls back again.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:24:Instead of reacting to instantaneous point-in-time metrics, the controller incorporates two fixed-size rolling windows:
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:25:* `controller_window_t`: Tracks `S3_global`, `S4`, and `S4_burst` over the last 10 accepted valid updates to average out noise.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:33:* Degradation requires at least 3 continuous updates in `NORMAL` where the moving average of `S3_global` or `S4` is below `CONTROLLER_THRESHOLD`.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:39:The controller records applied actuator movements. If the derivative of the actuator changes sign repeatedly without matching coordination improvement (4 or more reversals over the past 8 updates), an oscillation event is raised. Severe oscillation from `DEGRADED` automatically transitions the controller into `DISABLED`.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:43:A `last_known_good` vector is captured only when the controller has been in `NORMAL` for at least 5 updates without experiencing saturation or oscillation events. 
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:45:During `ROLLBACK`, the controller atomically applies the `last_known_good` vector. If no known-good vector was successfully recorded (e.g., fault at startup), the controller falls back to safe default constraints (`TRANSITION_REASON_DEFAULT_RECOVERY`).
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:55:To accommodate the complex diagnostic data (residence time, rollback causes, invalid frame counts, oscillation scores) without violating the strictly 83-column `v4` schema, a new explicitly versioned, append-only `orchestra.paper_cpu.metrics/v5` schema has been introduced.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:57:The `v5` schema introduces 24 new columns detailing the exact numeric state of the controller, requested vs. applied limits, transition reasons, and suppression mechanisms.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:63:No dynamic allocation, unbounded loops, or sleeping mechanisms occur inside the controller update logic.
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:67:* This controller represents an exploratory control-theory safeguard. It does not replace the Linux scheduler (`sched_ext`).
docs/adr/0007-controller-safety-state-machine.md:68:* Fallbacks triggered by this controller merely stabilize userspace behavior guidelines; hard real-time tasks are exempt from this flow entirely.
