docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:216:### 2.6 Prediction selection, last-known-good behavior, and fallback
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:218:Prediction fallback and signal-loss fallback are separate mechanisms.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:226:`prediction_used` records that choice in the authenticated payload and CSV output. This is observed-state fallback for predictor confidence; it does not bypass signal-frame verification.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:230:- if a last-known-good payload exists and its authenticated `max_age_ns` has not expired, the worker keeps its previously selected action and does not make a new decision from the rejected, absent, or unstable frame;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:231:- if no last-known-good payload exists, its timestamp is now in the future, or its age exceeds its `max_age_ns`, the worker records `THROTTLE` and sets `fallback_active`; and
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:232:- a later `FRAME_VALID` decision clears `fallback_active` and re-enters the adaptive path.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:234:The fallback action is the userspace `THROTTLE` approximation implemented as bounded busy work followed by sleep. It is not a transition to a different Linux scheduling class and is not a conventional Linux scheduler fallback. There is no independently acquired worker-local observation fallback after signal loss.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:236:Hard-real-time-designated workers enter a separate bypass loop and do not enter this adaptive verification or fallback chain. An unsuccessful `SCHED_FIFO` attempt is reported on stderr, but the worker remains classified as exempt and continues its deterministic userspace loop under the Linux policy actually granted.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:238:### 2.7 Implemented telemetry
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:243:- observed, predicted, and selected decision CPU values;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:244:- `prediction_used`, confidence, forecast error, and parent-observed frame age;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:246:- eligible action counts and eligible/fallback worker counts;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:247:- S1, S2, S3, S4, and geometric-mean Q;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:249:- controller update, step, reason, beta, saturation, and consensus-application fields;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:253:Startup diagnostics on stderr include configuration, effective random seed, signal schema, and state schema. `SCHED_FIFO` failure and environment/setup failures also use stderr, keeping diagnostics separate from CSV.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:255:Per-worker `accepted_sequence`, heartbeat, alive state, fallback state, and rejection count exist in shared state. Of these, accepted sequence contributes to S1 signal reach, fallback state contributes to `fallback_workers`, and rejection counts are summed for CSV.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:257:The generation-stamped transport also maintains bounded shared diagnostics for
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:258:publication attempts/successes/contention/generation exhaustion and for reader
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:267:current-frame acceptance count, controller-skip reason, key epoch, tier/source
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:269:fallback-reason changes, per-reader records, or a separate count of injected
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:279:- no core/socket/NUMA/system hierarchy beyond the one accepted local tier value;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:285:- no per-reader diagnostics or retry/reason counts in the paper-CPU CSV, sequence-gap telemetry, or component-level verification-latency telemetry in that CSV; bounded internal diagnostics and the separate v1 local microbenchmark protocol do not change the CSV contract;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:286:- no long-duration generation-exhaustion/soak evidence or general publication/verification overhead budget; the v1 protocol can record bounded local invocation-level snapshot-copy and full verified-read timings, but does not establish a performance result;
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:288:- no independent local-observation or conventional-scheduler fallback after signal expiry; and
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:295:The implementation supports the statement that the current single-host userspace prototype serializes a canonical 128-byte schema-1 payload, authenticates it with a separate full HMAC-SHA256 tag, and uses the bounded generation-stamped two-slot userspace transport described in ADR 0006 before a greater-sequence frame drives a new adaptive decision.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:309:### Retain the byte-wise versioned transport as the default
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:348:The paper's simulator reports detection of injected payload corruption and models stale information as reducing signal fidelity. It also identifies key distribution as unsolved and evaluates only one local coordination tier. Those simulation results motivate the mechanism but do not validate this userspace implementation or a production security property.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:351:deterministic big-endian encoding, a full-payload HMAC, the generation-stamped
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:353:validation, sequence classification, and last-known-good fallback behavior.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:355:cases together with bounded generation-stamped publication behavior. The unit
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:360:The [v1 local microbenchmark protocol](../experiments/signal_publication_microbenchmark_v1.md)
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:377:- The HMAC covers all 128 payload bytes, including schema, identity, sequence, age bound, directive, predictor metadata, observed/predicted state, and controller parameters.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:379:  slot generation, acquired token confirmation, and a reader pin obtained from
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:384:- Invalid, no-new, and unstable reads retain only the previous action while the last-known-good frame remains fresh; expiry selects the bounded userspace `THROTTLE` fallback.
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:395:snapshot attempt uses token/slot-generation checks, a reader-count pin, and up
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:415:the same selected publication mode. The default is generation-stamped; the
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:420:non-`1` signal schema, non-`2` state schema, nonlocal tier/source, or
docs/adr/0002-userspace-signal-frame-contract.md:425:Experiment records currently identify signal and state schema in startup diagnostics, while CSV rows use the fixed metrics header. The payload itself has no run identifier or manifest checksum. External experiment manifests and artifact provenance remain responsible for tying a run, binary, environment, and dataset together.
